<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Captaincy of Ilhéus</h1><p class="st-meta">1534 CE – 1761 CE · capital Ilhéus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A hereditary Portuguese captaincy on the coast of southern Bahia, troubled by indigenous wars, which much later became the heart of Brazil's cacao country.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-of-portugal">King John III</a> granted the Captaincy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilheus">Ilhéus</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-all-saints">Bay of All Saints</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-de-figueiredo-correia">Jorge de Figueiredo Correia</a>, a royal official who never went there. His lieutenant Francisco Romero founded the town of São Jorge dos Ilhéus around 1536.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The captaincy developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mills along the rivers, and for a time it was one of the more promising settlements. From the mid-16th century repeated attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aimore">Aimoré</a> people destroyed mills and drove settlers away, and the population remained small. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions were founded among indigenous groups. The captaincy passed through several proprietors, including the merchant Lucas Giraldes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Pombal</a>'s reforms abolishing private captaincies, the crown took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilheus">Ilhéus</a> in 1761, and it was later attached to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahia">Bahia</a>. In the 19th century the region became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil's</a> main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>-growing area, celebrated in the novels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jorge-amado">Jorge Amado</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1534 CE – 1761 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56 H50"/><pat